# NEW (the forwarding stand-ins). __olang_scope_merge(dst, src) does not resolve dst. When dst is a stand-in that was
# folded already (its head is @__olang_fwd_chunk), a later fold into it splices src's chunks and destructor nodes into
# that stand-in instead of into the scope it forwards to. The stand-in stops forwarding (its head is no longer the
# sentinel, its tail is still the forward pointer, and the sentinel global sits at the end of its chunk list), and the
# stand-in is never closed. Everything later built into it - by the task, and by the main thread calling the kept
# closure, which now allocates there directly - is never freed, and its destructors never run.
# Shape: the closure k a first task handed back holds the first task's stand-in S1 (folded, forwarding to run's scope).
# A second task is handed k, gets a stand-in S2 whose parent is S1, builds R(2), and S2 is folded into S1 at the join.
#     expected: "end of run", then dtor 4, dtor 3 and dtor 2 in some order, then "after run" (master)
#     actual:   -b, -d -s and -r: only "dtor 4" (R(2) and R(3) never destructed)
# The same through os.RunOnStack (h3b): RunOnStack(1 << 20, k) with k kept past a join loses dtor 1 and dtor 2.
type R struct(id I64) {
    id
} destruct {
    println("dtor " $id)
}
type Holder struct() {
    res mut R& = null
}
fn pass(f fn(i I64)) fn(i I64) { return f }
fn call(f fn(i I64), i I64) { f(i) }
fn run() {
    n mut Holder& = Holder()
    g := fn(i I64) { n.res = R(i) }
    k fn(i I64) = g
    join {
        spawn k = pass(g)
    }
    join {
        spawn call(k, 2)
    }
    k(3)
    g(4)
    println("end of run")
}
fn main() ? {
    run()
    println("after run")
}
